            <ArticleTitle>Osteophyte at the sacroiliac joint as a cause of sciatica: a report of four cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>73-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Four cases of sciatica due to osteophytes impinging on the sciatic nerve at the sacroiliac joint are reported. Of these 4 cases, 2 were treated conservatively and the other 2 required surgical excision of the osteophyte. The report highlights the importance of keeping this uncommon etiology in the differential diagnosis of sciatica.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
